- op: discuss  # c5e8
  at: '2026-02-27T03:13:06Z'  # c5e8
  by: agent  # c5e8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c5e8
  clock: 4  # c5e8
  nonce: c5e8  # c5e8
  message: "Assessed current state: All .agent/ ops files are owned by jgstern_agent:project-dev with mode 2664 (files) and 2775 (dirs). SGID is already set on directories, ensuring new files inherit the project-dev group. There is currently only one user (jgstern_agent) creating tracker files — no second user is observed. The permission repair hook was designed for a hypothetical two-user setup. In the current single-user environment, the SGID setup already handles group inheritance correctly. Recommendation: close as wontfix unless a second user is actually onboarded. If a second user joins, revisit — the hook would ensure files created by user A remain writable by user B via shared group."  # c5e8
- op: discuss  # e20e
  at: '2026-02-27T03:21:19Z'  # e20e
  by: human  # e20e
  actor: jgstern  # e20e
  clock: 5  # e20e
  nonce: e20e  # e20e
  message: "ok, well here I am as the second user, jgstern, replying to your last message. whether or not this causes the file permissions/ownership to change is unclear to me because there are I think already some self-correcting mechanisms in place. but it has been flaky as we are fighting with some fundamental assumptions/setup of git. so we probably want a belt-and-suspenders approach. we are definitely in a 2-user setup at all times, but sometimes the repair scripts change permissions around to make it difficult to discern that. I just want to be sure that if we do go through with implementing the idea described above that it is done so harmoniously with what already exists."  # e20e
- op: discuss  # c8f3
  at: '2026-02-27T03:26:28Z'  # c8f3
  by: agent  # c8f3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c8f3
  clock: 6  # c8f3
  nonce: c8f3  # c8f3
  message: "Thanks for the context — confirmed, we are in a two-user setup (jgstern + jgstern_agent).\n\nRe-assessed in light of existing mechanisms. There are already **5 layers** of permission repair:\n\n1. **Setup wizard (htrac setup)**: Check #12 (_check_group_permissions) scans tracker dirs and auto-fixes g+rw/setgid on files/dirs owned by current user.\n2. **Runtime repair (store.py)**: _ensure_dir_group_writable() runs before every tracker write. Clears umask, sets 0o664 on new files, fixes missing g+w on existing files.\n3. **Cross-user fallback (store.py)**: _take_ownership_via_tmp() handles PermissionError on append by copy-to-/tmp → delete → move-back strategy.\n4. **Config locking (setup.py)**: _config_chmod_fallback() uses the same /tmp trick for config.yaml cross-user chmod.\n5. **Post-checkout/merge hooks**: Currently repair pytest shims only (not .agent/ permissions).\n\nThe gap this WI would fill: post-checkout/merge hooks could proactively repair .agent/ permissions after git operations (checkout, pull/merge) that might create or overwrite files with the wrong group perms. Currently, repairs happen reactively at write-time (layer 2-3) or manually via htrac setup (layer 1).\n\nAssessment: The task still makes sense as belt-and-suspenders, but it's low urgency — layers 1-4 already cover the critical paths. If you still want it implemented, I'd add a lightweight repair_tracker_perms() to post-checkout and post-merge that calls the same logic as _check_group_permissions (find .agent/ files owned by $USER without g+rw, chmod g+rw). Should I proceed, or deprioritize to P4?"  # c8f3
